== Storyline notes ==

* '''Marcus Vance's organic babyface turn is complete.''' Vance entered the Aizawa Dome as a tweener — still associated with the Cannon Club, still cold, but increasingly sympathetic after being abandoned at Episode 6. By the end of the night, he had slapped Jusuf Muntasir, cut a promo defending professional wrestling and the fans, and been cheered out of the building. The Cannon Club did not appear during the main event. Vance is now alone — and for the first time, the crowd is with him. The question for Island Invasion: does Vance officially leave the Cannon Club, or does Kengo try to pull him back?
* '''Jusuf Muntasir establishes himself as a top heel.''' The Baliscan oil wrestler dominated long stretches of the Intercontinental Championship match with his unusual grappling style, then used a low blow to cause a disqualification. His post-match attack and the Abrazo de Terra showed he is not a respectful outsider — he is a contemptuous invader. The slap from Vance sets up a highly personal rematch at Island Invasion. Jusuf's first loss is a DQ, so he remains protected.
* '''The Cannon Club's silence on Vance.''' Kengo, Malo, the Usos, and the rest of the faction did not come to Vance's aid during or after the Jusuf attack. This was a deliberate choice — the faction watched one of its champions get assaulted and did nothing. The storyline implication is that Kengo views Vance's growing popularity as a threat, or that the Cannon Club's loyalty only extends to those who win cleanly. Either way, the fracture is now public.
* '''Leoni sees the shift.''' The backstage segment with Leoni watching Vance's promo confirms that the Tribal Chief recognizes Vance's turn before the rest of the Cannon Club does. This plants the seed for a potential alliance or mutual respect between Leoni and Vance down the line — two men who understand what it means to carry a title alone.
* '''The Vaiahi family war.''' Leoni and Taumafa defeated Kengo and Malo in the family war tag match. The win gives Leoni and Taumafa momentum, but Kengo's stare-down after the match showed he is not shaken. At Fangatapu, the family hierarchy will be decided in separate matches — Leoni against KAZUKI for the IGO Heavyweight Championship, and Taumafa defending the Strong Openweight Championship against a challenger to be named.
* '''KAZUKI's path of destruction.''' KAZUKI defeated Daigo Kuroki — his second consecutive win over a former Kuroshio member after beating Ryohei Maeda at Episode 6. He has now defeated both of Leoni's former brothers. The message to Leoni: there is no one left to save you. KAZUKI's isolation is absolute, and his focus on the title is total.
* '''The young guns continue their war of attrition.''' Ono and Yamamoto defeated two Junior Tag Champions. Sato pushed Tanaka hard in a TV title match. Iwata fell to The Marauder, but the young core is clearly ascending. The Cannon Club's junior division is under siege.
* '''Tag team title match becomes a street fight.''' The no contest between the Usos and Reyes & Kimura — with the pre-bell attack and arena-wide brawl — established that the Island Invasion match will be violent and personal. The Usos have retained twice by non-clean finishes, making them look vulnerable despite holding the titles.
* '''The Marauder remains undefeated.''' Four consecutive Wednesday night wins. The mysterious powerhouse is becoming a problem that NOPW will have to address after Island Invasion.
* '''The Aizawa Dome sellout.''' 10,500 tickets sold in 11 hours, breaking the Ogasawara record. The Wednesday night product has now sold out seven consecutive shows in seven different markets: Nagayama (4,200), Kurosaki (6,500), Tosa (6,000), Naruto (7,500), Okayama (8,500), Ogasawara (9,000), and Aizawa (10,500). The growth is unprecedented. The next stop is Fangatapu, Vaiahi Islands — the biggest event of the NOPW calendar.

